186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 84 0 R
/T (Executive Summary)
/E (Executive Summary)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (This report examines how Dipped Products PLC \(DPL\), a leading rubber glove manufacturer in the Hayleys Group, can use the Internet of Things \(IoT\) and Artificial Intelligence \(AI\) to improve its manufacturing process. It is based on a field visit to the DPL factory in Kottawa, where the production process was observed from raw material handling to final packing.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
188 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (The visit showed that DPL already collects a significant amount of data through its Enterprise Resource Planning \(ERP\) system, laboratory records, machine logs, and quality checkpoints. However, much of this data is still used to support human decision-making rather than automated or intelligent responses. Two areas stood out during the visit and are used as case studies throughout this report. The first is compound mixing, where formulation errors may only be identified after the required maturation period and laboratory testing. The second is manual quality control, where worker fatigue can reduce inspection accuracy and increase the risk of defects being missed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
189 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (A phased approach is recommended. First, computer vision should be introduced to support manual quality inspection and identify visible defects. This should be followed by automated counting and digital verification in packaging, particularly for high-volume and standardised pack types. The third phase should focus on mixing through inline sensors and automated dosing, while keeping important adjustments under human approval. Once reliable data has been established across these areas, an Artificial Intelligence combined with IoT \(AIoT\) layer can be introduced to identify patterns, predict potential problems, and provide decision recommendations.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (The report also considers the business case, implementation approach, and workforce impact of these changes. The main constraint is not expected to be investment capacity, but the speed of implementation, system integration, and operational risk. Critical safety checks, such as the pinhole and voltage-leak test, should remain deterministic and should not be replaced by probabilistic AI decisions. Automation should also be supported by retraining and role rotation so that affected workers can transition into suitable roles rather than being viewed only as a reduction in headcount. This approach would allow DPL to gradually move from collecting production data to using it for faster, more informed, and predictive decision-making.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 84 0 R
/Pg 12 0 R
/A [528 0 R 0]
/K [8]
>>
endobj
