198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (This report is based on a field visit made by our team to the Dipped Products PLC \(DPL\) factory in Kottawa. The purpose of the visit was to understand the rubber glove manufacturing process and identify where technologies such as the Internet of Things \(IoT\) and Artificial Intelligence combined with IoT \(AIoT\) could be used to improve the process. The report documents the observations made during the visit, highlights stages where the process remains heavily dependent on manual work, and proposes practical improvements based on feasibility, cost, and workforce impact.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 85 0 R
/T (1.1 Purpose and Scope)
/E (1.1 Purpose and Scope)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (DPL was established in 1976 and is the oldest and one of the largest plants within its group, which today includes 13 plants producing around 5 million pairs of gloves. DPL is part of the Hayleys Group, one of Sri Lanka's largest and most diversified companies, and is itself listed on the Colombo Stock Exchange. It is recognised as one of the world's largest glove manufacturers, with exports reaching more than 70 countries. The company is supported by its own Research and Development \(R&D\) team, rubber plantations, and manufacturing plants located in Sri Lanka and Thailand.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 85 0 R
/T (1.2 DPL and the Hayleys Group)
/E (1.2 DPL and the Hayleys Group)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (The basic glove manufacturing process begins with a hand-shaped mould known as a former, which is dipped into a tank containing liquid rubber, such as natural latex or synthetic materials such as nitrile or neoprene. The former is first heated before being dipped, and the process may be repeated to achieve the required glove thickness. The rubber layer is then dried to form the glove. Once formed, the gloves are subjected to quality checks, testing, and cleaning before being packed and shipped. The raw materials are also tested before being allowed into the factory to ensure that they meet DPL's quality standards.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 85 0 R
/T (1.3 How Rubber Gloves Are Made)
/E (1.3 How Rubber Gloves Are Made)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (2. Factory Floor Observations)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 85 0 R
/T (2.1 Raw Material Sourcing and Incoming Quality Control)
/E (2.1 Raw Material Sourcing and Incoming Quality Control)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (Each batch of raw material is tested before use, regardless of supplier data. A colour tag system is used to show the status of each batch. Green indicates passed, blue indicates on hold, and orange indicates not yet checked.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (Material that fails specifications may be adjusted and approved through a signed Non-Conformance Report \(NCR\) or returned to the supplier. For example, a material with a Total Solids Content \(TSC\) reading of 40.9 was approved following technical review despite being below specification. Imported chemicals and pigments account for approximately 99.9% of usage and are mainly flammable, so they are stored externally.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (Although largely manual, the process is structured and rule-based, providing a contrast to compound mixing.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 85 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [533 0 R 0]
/K [17]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 86 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/A [534 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 86 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/A [535 0 R 0]
/K [1]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (03)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (Chemicals are combined according to batch card standards recorded in SAP. Quantities can be specified with high precision. For example, a 500 g component may be required within a 6,000 kg batch. Dosing is therefore controlled through SAP and materials are pumped into a pressurised tank.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (However, individual chemicals must first be lab-tested, and the mixed compound requires a maturation period of up to 3.5 days for some formulations. Further laboratory testing is then carried out for viscosity, colour, TSC, and pH before production approval.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
