247 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (DPL already collects substantial data through laboratory results, order specifications, machine logs, and quality control records. Much of this is recorded through the Enterprise Resource Planning \(ERP\) system, although some paper records remain. The main gap is therefore not the availability of data, but how it is used.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (When process parameters drift, batches are placed on hold, or defect trends develop, the data is still reviewed by a person before action is taken. Data currently supports human decision-making but does not independently trigger responses.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (The recommendations are therefore divided into two stages. First, an Internet of Things \(IoT\) layer should improve real-time sensing, automated data collection, and data reliability. An Artificial Intelligence combined with IoT \(AIoT\) layer can then be introduced to analyse this data and support intelligent decisions.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Artificial Intelligence should not be introduced before the underlying data and sensing issues are addressed, as unreliable data could lead to unreliable decisions. A strong IoT foundation is therefore necessary for the AIoT layer.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
