/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Once the quality control and packaging improvements are established, the mixing process should be strengthened with inline sensors on maturation and feed tanks. Continuous monitoring of viscosity, pH, and Total Solids Content \(TSC\) would allow deviations to be detected during the process instead of after several days of maturation and laboratory testing. Industrial refractometers such as the Vaisala Polaris PR53 are already used for continuous concentration monitoring in rubber and latex applications.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (For small chemical adjustments, a Human-in-the-Loop \(HITL\) dosing process is recommended. When an abnormal reading is detected, the system could calculate the required adjustment and send it to a responsible executive for approval. Once approved, automated pumps could deliver the required amount, while the Enterprise Resource Planning \(ERP\) system records the adjustment and approver.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Similar automated weighing and dosing systems are already used in rubber compounding to improve recipe accuracy and traceability. DPL could therefore build on its existing SAP-based process rather than replacing it.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 89 0 R
