/Pg 17 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Two sensor types are particularly relevant to the mixing process. Optical refractometers such as the Vaisala Polaris can continuously measure concentration through the refractive index of the material, while vibrating-rod viscometers can continuously measure viscosity. This would provide more immediate process information than periodic laboratory testing.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (When connected to DPL's existing Programmable Logic Controllers \(PLCs\) and ERP system, these sensors could identify deviations and recommend adjustments before a batch is fully committed. For example, a drop in coagulant concentration caused by water carryover could trigger an adjustment recommendation, with final approval remaining under human control.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (The existing laboratory testing could therefore be retained as a quality safeguard while the IoT system reduces the likelihood of major deviations being discovered only after maturation. The data generated across quality control, packaging, and mixing would then provide the foundation for the predictive AIoT layer proposed in the final phase.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
271 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (4.4 Specific Sensor Options for the Mixing Retrofit)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
272 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Such systems are already used in glove manufacturing, where automated counting, cartoning, and barcode or Radio Frequency Identification \(RFID\) verification are used to improve packing accuracy and traceability.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
