278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (The Artificial Intelligence combined with the Internet of Things \(AIoT\) layer should be introduced only after reliable data has been established across quality control, packaging, and mixing. At this stage, data from sensors, inspection systems, production records, and the Enterprise Resource Planning \(ERP\) system could be analysed together to identify patterns and predict potential problems.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (For example, historical quality data could be used to identify defect trends across machines and shifts before they become major quality issues. Similarly, mixing data such as viscosity, pH, Total Solids Content \(TSC\), and temperature could be analysed to identify conditions associated with unstable batches. Machine learning has already been studied for predicting rubber compound properties such as tensile strength, showing potential for predictive applications in this industry.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (The same data could also support predictive decisions in packaging and quality control. Instead of responding only after a defect, delay, or process deviation occurs, the system could identify early warning patterns and recommend appropriate action.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 90 0 R
/T (5.1 Predictive Decision-Making Across the Production Process)
/E (5.1 Predictive Decision-Making Across the Production Process)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (The final AIoT layer should not be designed to make every decision independently. A Human-in-the-Loop \(HITL\) approach should be maintained for decisions that could affect production, quality, or safety.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (When an unusual pattern is detected, the system could provide a prediction or recommended action to the responsible employee. The decision could then be reviewed and approved before any significant process adjustment is made. This would allow AI to support technical staff without removing human responsibility from important decisions.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (For example, if mixing data indicates that a batch is likely to move outside its required viscosity range, the system could recommend an adjustment based on previous production data. The responsible executive could review the recommendation before automated dosing is allowed.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 90 0 R
/T (5.2 Human-in-the-Loop Decision-Making)
/E (5.2 Human-in-the-Loop Decision-Making)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Different production problems require different technologies. The existing electrical test can identify pinholes affecting electrical insulation, while computer vision can identify visible scratches, tears, or other surface defects. Similarly, mixing sensors provide direct measurements that AI can later use for prediction.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (A combination of purpose-built technologies should therefore be used rather than relying on one AI system for the entire production process. AI should be applied where it provides a clear advantage, while physical sensors, deterministic tests, and human inspection should remain where they are more reliable.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (5.3 A Portfolio of Methods Rather Than One AI System)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
