/E (09)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
316 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (The final investment stage should be the development of the Artificial Intelligence combined with the Internet of Things \(AIoT\) layer. By this stage, reliable data would be available from quality control, packaging, mixing, and other production activities.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (This data could be used to identify trends, predict potential failures, and recommend actions before problems affect production. The financial value would therefore come not only from reducing individual errors, but from improving overall process stability and reducing unexpected downtime.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
318 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (The AIoT system should remain Human-in-the-Loop, particularly where decisions could affect product quality or production. Critical safety checks should continue to use deterministic methods rather than AI-based probability.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (6.4 Predictive AIoT)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (A phased approach would reduce the financial and operational risk of introducing automation across the factory. Each stage would generate practical results and better-quality data that can support the following stage.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
321 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (The main constraint is therefore not expected to be capital availability, but implementation speed, system integration, and workforce transition. Automation should be supported by retraining and role transition so that affected workers can move into areas such as equipment monitoring, quality verification, and maintenance support.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 92 0 R
/T (6.5 Overall Investment Consideration)
/E (6.5 Overall Investment Consideration)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (DPL already has an in-house product development team, and funding was not identified as the main barrier to implementation. The greater priority should therefore be placed on execution speed, system integration, and avoiding disruption to daily production. Implementation should follow the same phased approach recommended in this report, beginning with computer vision, followed by packaging counting, mixing instrumentation, and finally predictive Artificial Intelligence combined with the Internet of Things \(AIoT\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (A hybrid implementation approach is recommended. DPL's internal team should lead areas requiring detailed knowledge of the factory and its existing systems, while external specialists can be engaged for technical areas such as computer vision, sensor integration, and machine learning. This would allow specialist knowledge to be introduced while operational knowledge remains within DPL.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (A pilot-based approach should be followed rather than implementing large-scale systems at once. The computer vision system should first be tested on a selected production line, followed by automated counting for suitable packaging types. Mixing sensors and dosing systems can then be introduced and connected to the existing systems. Once reliable data has been established across these areas, predictive AIoT applications can be developed.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (This gradual approach is particularly important because previous automation efforts, including the introduction of robotic arms, were affected by configuration issues that took more than a month to resolve. Each stage should therefore be tested under actual production conditions before wider deployment. External partners should also train DPL's internal team so that the technologies can eventually be managed and improved internally without long-term dependence on vendors.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 92 0 R
/T (7. Implementation Layer)
/E (7. Implementation Layer)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
