>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 93 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/A [564 0 R 0]
/K [1]
>>
endobj
331 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (10)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
332 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (The introduction of automation would affect workers currently involved in quality inspection and packaging, particularly contract workers. However, workforce reduction should not be treated as the primary objective of automation. A transition plan should instead be included from the beginning of the implementation process.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Computer vision should initially be used to support quality inspectors rather than replace them. Similarly, packaging automation should be introduced gradually, allowing time for affected workers to be reassigned or retrained. As identified by management, opportunities exist within other areas of the company where contract workers could be rotated as automation is introduced.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Training could be provided for roles involving machine monitoring, quality verification, equipment support, and other activities that continue to require human involvement. Human oversight should also be retained for important AIoT decisions, particularly where product quality or safety could be affected.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (This approach would allow DPL to gain the benefits of automation while retaining the practical knowledge and experience of its workforce. Automation should therefore be treated as a transition in roles and responsibilities rather than simply a reduction in jobs.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
336 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 93 0 R
/T (8. Ethical Considerations and Workforce Transition)
/E (8. Ethical Considerations and Workforce Transition)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (DPL has already demonstrated its ability to adopt advanced automation, particularly through its robotic dipping operations. However, mixing, quality control, and packing still depend heavily on manual work. The main opportunity is therefore not simply to introduce more technology, but to connect the data already being generated with systems that can support faster and more consistent decisions.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (A four-phase roadmap is recommended:)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
339 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Phase 1: Computer Vision for Quality Control. A computer vision system should first be introduced to support manual inspection by detecting surface-level defects. Human inspectors should remain involved in reviewing uncertain cases, while critical tests such as the pinhole test should remain fully deterministic.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Phase 2: Automated Counting and Packaging Support. Automated counting and digital verification should then be introduced to reduce manual counting errors and improve packing accuracy. This can initially be applied to the most standardised and high-volume pack types before wider packaging automation is considered.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Phase 3: Automated Mixing and Dosing. Inline sensors should be introduced to monitor mixing conditions, with automated dosing systems used to prepare small chemical adjustments. The existing SAP system can be connected to these processes so that recipe accuracy and traceability are maintained, while final adjustments remain subject to human approval.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Phase 4: Predictive AIoT Decision-Making. Once reliable data is being collected across quality control, packing, mixing, and other production stages, Artificial Intelligence combined with IoT \(AIoT\) can be introduced to identify patterns and predict potential problems. Human-in-the-Loop \(HITL\) controls should remain in place so that important decisions are reviewed and authorised by responsible personnel.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (The overall objective is to move DPL from a factory that mainly collects data to one that uses its data to act earlier and more intelligently. At every stage, safety and workforce protection should remain priorities. Critical safety decisions should not be based solely on AI probabilities, and automation should be accompanied by retraining and role transition plans for affected workers.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 93 0 R
/T (9. Conclusion and Phased Recommendations)
/E (9. Conclusion and Phased Recommendations)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
